# Off-site backup destination — copy to /root/.config/alo/backup.env (0600).
#
# The nightly repository lives on the same filesystem as the data it protects.
# That covers every failure which leaves the machine standing, and none that
# takes the disk with it. Set a destination here and each backup is copied to
# it; restic sends only what the destination lacks, so a nightly run costs the
# day's changes.
#
# Any restic backend works. Some examples:
#
#   OFFSITE_REPOSITORY=sftp:backup@another-host:/srv/alo-backups
#   OFFSITE_REPOSITORY=s3:s3.eu-central-003.backblazeb2.com/alo-backups
#   OFFSITE_REPOSITORY=rest:https://user:pass@backup.example.eu/alo/
#
# A second EU location is what the product description promises customers, so
# prefer a destination that keeps that promise rather than one that is merely
# elsewhere.
#
# If the destination uses a different password from the local repository:
#   OFFSITE_PASSWORD_FILE=/root/.config/alo/offsite-password
#
# Backend credentials go here too, e.g. for S3-compatible storage:
#   AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...
#   AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...

OFFSITE_REPOSITORY=

# If no destination is set, the monitor reports the gap every six hours. That is
# correct while it can be acted on and corrosive when it cannot — an alert
# nobody can fix teaches whoever reads these emails to ignore all of them. So it
# can be acknowledged, but only with an end date, after which it speaks up
# again. An acknowledgement that expires is a decision; one that does not is a
# way of forgetting.
#
#   OFFSITE_ACKNOWLEDGED_UNTIL=2026-09-30
